.\" Manpage for airpods-control.
.TH AIRPODS-CONTROL 1 "July 29, 2026" "airpods-control 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
airpods-control \- control AirPods listening mode and Conversation Awareness
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B airpods-control
.RB [ --device
.IR name ]
.RB ( listening-mode | lm )
.RB ( get | list )
.RB [ --json ]
.RB [ --debug ]
.br
.B airpods-control
.RB [ --device
.IR name ]
.RB ( listening-mode | lm )
.BI "set " mode
.RB [ --json ]
.RB [ --debug ]
.br
.B airpods-control
.RB [ --device
.IR name ]
.RB ( listening-mode | lm )
.B cycle
.RB [ --modes
.IR m1,m2[,...] ]
.RB [ --json ]
.RB [ --debug ]
.br
.B airpods-control
.RB [ --device
.IR name ]
.RB ( conversation-awareness | ca )
.B get
.RB [ --json ]
.RB [ --debug ]
.br
.B airpods-control
.RB [ --device
.IR name ]
.RB ( conversation-awareness | ca )
.BI "set " "on|off"
.RB [ --json ]
.RB [ --debug ]
.br
.B airpods-control
.B support-report
.RB [ --with-write-tests " | " --no-write-tests ]
.br
.B airpods-control
.RB [ --version | -v | version ]
.RB [ --help | -h ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B airpods-control
reads and sets the AirPods listening mode and Conversation Awareness by talking
directly to the macOS system audio daemon through a private AVFoundation API
.RB ( AVOutputDevice
in
.BR AVRouting.framework ).
A change takes effect immediately and shows the same on-screen banner as a stem
press. No Control Center, menu bar, or Accessibility permission is involved.
.PP
Reaching the API requires forging one private audio entitlement
.RB ( com.apple.avfoundation.allow-system-wide-context )
inside the
.B airpods-control
process via a small interpose library inserted with
.BR DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES .
See the project README and SECURITY document for the full trust model.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS LISTENING MODE
.TP
.BR "listening-mode get" ", " "lm get"
Print the current listening mode.
.TP
.BR "listening-mode set " mode ", " "lm set " mode
Set the listening mode. If the mode is already active, prints
.B ok
without issuing a write. Otherwise prints
.B ok
on a verified change, or
.B no-op
if the requested mode is not observed within the bounded verification window.
.TP
.BR "listening-mode list" ", " "lm list"
Print the comma-separated modes supported by the connected device in the
deterministic order documented under
.BR MODES .
.TP
.BR "listening-mode cycle" ", " "lm cycle"
Advance to the next mode in the cycle set, wrapping around, and print the mode
landed on. Modes always cycle in the order documented under
.BR MODES .
The cycle set defaults to every mode the device supports except
.BR off ;
.B --modes
selects an explicit subset of at least two distinct modes. Modes the device
does not support are skipped; if fewer than two remain the command reports
.B unsupported
(exit 4). If the current mode is outside the cycle set (including
.BR off ),
cycle still advances in canonical order from the current mode to the next mode
that is in the set, wrapping around; if the current mode is unknown, cycle
starts at the set's first mode. A change that does not verify reports
.B no-op
(exit 3).
.SS CONVERSATION AWARENESS
.TP
.BR "conversation-awareness get" ", " "ca get"
Print the current Conversation Awareness state as
.B on
or
.BR off .
.TP
.BR "conversation-awareness set " "on|off" ", " "ca set " "on|off"
Set Conversation Awareness. The setter is idempotent and uses the same
.B ok
and
.B no-op
contract as listening mode.
.SS GLOBAL
.TP
.B support-report
Build a local compatibility report from existing device and macOS metadata.
Exactly one compatible output device must be available. The command does not
read device names or choose arbitrarily among devices.
The read-only report includes only the normalized model identifier, the
advertised listening modes, the advertised Conversation Awareness capability,
whether the listening-mode and Conversation Awareness queries answer, whether
macOS exposes their setters, the macOS version, and the CLI version. The model
name is resolved locally from the Bluetooth product ID embedded in the model
identifier. Advertised listening modes that this CLI version does not
recognize are listed verbatim, capped at six, and filtered through the
report's character allowlist. Missing data is marked unavailable or not
reported. The command does not guess it.
.IP
The read-only report does not include the setting values returned by the
queries and never invokes a setter. A consented run reads setting values
locally only to plan, verify, and restore its writes. If restoration cannot be
verified, the report names the final state so it can be restored manually. The
prefilled Compatibility report field includes the same per-mode verdicts but
omits the restoration outcome.
.IP
When at least one write test can be planned safely, an interactive run captures
the initial settings and advertised capabilities, displays that plan, and asks
for consent, defaulting to no. The captured plan does not change after it is
disclosed. If a setting changes while consent is pending, that setting is
skipped rather than replaced with a different write.
.IP
Listening-mode tests cover only the advertised modes recognized by this CLI.
They attempt each noninitial mode and restore the captured initial mode last if
the state changed. All listening-mode writes are skipped if the setter is
missing, the initial mode is unreadable or not advertised, or there is no
alternate recognized advertised mode to test. Each completed listening-mode
write is held for about two seconds. Conversation Awareness is toggled away
from the captured initial state and back; it is skipped if its setter or
initial state is unavailable.
.IP
After normal completion or a setter error, the command makes one restoration
attempt if needed. Accepted but unverified writes are reported as
.BR no-op ;
setter rejections are reported as
.B setter error
and stop the remaining tests for that setting. Restoration setter errors and
restoration no-ops remain distinct in the report. A write whose target already
equals the state read immediately before it cannot demonstrate a transition;
if its readback still matches, it is reported as
.B "verified (already in this state; no transition demonstrated)"
rather than bare
.BR verified .
An unverified restoration
names the final state, gives a manual-fix hint, and exits 3.
.IP
An externally delivered SIGHUP, SIGINT, or SIGTERM caught during the tests
stops further writes, prints
.B "Interrupt caught; restoring initial settings..."
on standard error, attempts restoration first, prints any restoration warning,
and then exits 129, 130, or 143, respectively. SIGKILL, a process crash, or
power loss cannot guarantee restoration. The CLI does not generate
thread-directed signals; those are outside this process-signal guarantee. An
interrupted run does not offer or print an issue-form URL.
.IP
The prefilled GitHub Compatibility report field includes every per-mode verdict
but omits the restoration status and local creation note. The issue form
supplies the compatibility heading and keeps optional notes separate. Neither
report labels the row that restored the initial state, and when the state never
left the captured initial mode, that mode's untested listening-mode set row is
rendered without naming the mode.
.B --with-write-tests
answers the consent question with yes and is the only way to run the tests
noninteractively;
.B --no-write-tests
answers it with no.
.IP
It never reads the customizable device name, firmware version, serial numbers,
Bluetooth/MAC addresses, account data, or raw system dumps and logs. A
read-only report does not change device settings or intentionally interrupt
audio. Consented write tests temporarily change the settings in the captured
plan. The command never uses the clipboard, sends telemetry, or submits
anything.
.IP
Because
.B support-report
neither reads customizable names nor accepts
.BR --device ,
it requires exactly one compatible output device. With zero or multiple
compatible devices it exits 1 before a report, prompt, or write.
.IP
After any completed write-test prompt and run, the command prints the report
before asking whether to open a GitHub issue form. The form keeps the generated
Compatibility report separate from optional notes and requires an unchecked
privacy-review confirmation. It adds the compatibility label and assigns the
issue to raulgg. The contributor still reviews and submits the issue.
.IP
The CLI selects compatibility-report.yml, prefills the dynamic title, and
prefills the generated report through the form field ID report. If the encoded
URL is too long, the terminal prints a separate paste-ready GitHub Markdown
block and the same form opens with the title but without the report field.
.IP
The issue-opening question is asked only when standard input is interactive.
For a completed run under a script, pipeline, or CI, the command prints the
report and writes the ready-to-open issue form URL to standard error without
prompting or opening a browser, then returns the report outcome: normally 0,
or 3 if consented write tests could not restore the initial settings. Unless
the length cap above applies, that URL carries the generated report field with
write-test verdicts but without restoration status.
.IP
If the command cannot select one unique identifiable AirPods or Beats device,
it prints local instructions and stops. The CLI has only been verified with
AirPods Pro 3.
Other AirPods may work when macOS exposes the same private audio capabilities.
Beats reports are welcome, but Beats support has not been verified.
.TP
.BR --version ", " -v ", " version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR --help ", " -h
Print global or resource-specific usage and exit. Help may appear anywhere in
a command, takes precedence over all operations, and never accesses the device.
.SH MODES
.B off
(Normal/no active mode),
.BR transparency ,
.BR adaptive ,
.BR noise-cancellation .
.PP
For input,
.B trans
aliases
.BR transparency ;
.B automatic
and
.B auto
alias
.BR adaptive ;
and
.B anc
and
.B nc
alias
.BR noise-cancellation .
Output always uses canonical names. There is no alias for
.BR off .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--modes " m1,m2[,...]
For
.BR "listening-mode cycle" :
a comma-separated subset of the modes documented under
.BR MODES ,
at least two distinct entries. Mode aliases are accepted. Duplicates are
ignored. Unknown tokens are
.B bad-args
(exit 2).
.TP
.BI "--device " name
Target a compatible output device using a case-insensitive exact name match.
The option is accepted anywhere in an operational command and may be supplied
once. It never falls back to another device. No match or multiple exact matches
produce
.BR no-device .
.TP
.B --json
Emit structured JSON instead of plain script-friendly output. Accepted anywhere
in an operational command or global version command. Every JSON response has a
.B result
field. Device operations also contain
.B device
and the command's resulting
.B listeningMode
or
.B conversationAwareness
state; unavailable values are JSON
.BR null .
The listening-mode list command additionally returns
.BR supportedListeningModes .
.TP
.B --debug
Emit diagnostic logs to stderr without changing stdout, JSON, or the exit code.
Diagnostics cover the entitlement bypass, framework and selector discovery,
device selection, raw state, capability checks, writes, and read-back attempts.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
No supported target was selected, or no unique identifiable report device was
available (no-device).
.TP
.B 2
Missing or malformed arguments (bad-args).
.TP
.B 3
A requested write was not verified within the bounded window, or the
support-report write tests could not restore the initial settings (no-op).
.TP
.B 4
The mode or feature is not available on this device (unsupported).
.TP
.B 129
An externally delivered SIGHUP was caught during the write tests and
restoration was attempted.
.TP
.B 130
An externally delivered SIGINT was caught during the write tests and
restoration was attempted.
.TP
.B 143
An externally delivered SIGTERM was caught during the write tests and
restoration was attempted.
.SH NOTES
Listening-mode writes are checked every 50 milliseconds while the device
settles. Modes other than
.B off
return when the target is observed, within about 800 milliseconds. Changed
.B off
writes use a 1.5-second window because their fallback can bounce between modes.
If
.B off
is not verified, the setter accepted the request, and the device advertises
Transparency, direct writes and explicit cycles into
.B off
report
.B no-op
(exit 3) with
.B listeningMode
set to
.BR transparency .
This is the expected eventual fallback when Off Listening Mode is disabled,
not an observed final sample. With
.BR --debug ,
.B verify.listening_mode.inferred_off_fallback=true
identifies the inference. Rejected writes and devices without Transparency
report the final observed canonical mode, or
.B null
when the final readback is missing or unknown.
.PP
This relies on a private API and targets macOS Tahoe (26); Apple may change it
in any update. The tool probes known shared-system-audio context selector
variants and checks every required selector before invocation. It fails safe
(reporting
.B unsupported
or
.BR no-device )
rather than crashing when selectors are missing.
.PP
.B support-report
accepts no
.BR --device ,
.BR --json ,
or
.B --debug
option. Its only options are
.B --with-write-tests
and
.BR --no-write-tests .
The write-test consent prompt is device-specific; report result rows depend on
the captured plan and its outcomes.
